# Rust Stratified Modules

Rust mirror of the Clojure Layer-heading discipline (`languages/clojure`
210) and the Python component rule (221): inside one file, the item
graph is a one-way DAG of abstraction, marked with comment headings the
linter reads. Applies `foundations/stratified-design` (001) at file
granularity; crate-level layering stays with `foundations/layered-architecture`
(011) and rust.mdc's crate-structure rules.

## Marker

```rust
// ------------------------------------------------------------------ Layer 0
fn vocab() -> u32 { 1 }
// ------------------------------------------------------------------ Layer 1
fn top() -> u32 { vocab() }
```

- Comment heading: `//` + ≥4 dashes + `Layer N`. Chosen over a
  `#[stratum(n)]` attribute deliberately — an attribute would force a
  proc-macro build-dependency into every consuming crate; a heading is
  data the linter reads with zero compiler impact.
- Layer 0 is the file's vocabulary (types, consts, pure helpers); each
  higher layer is a small language over the ones below.
- **Calls are strict**: a reference whose target is a same-file `fn`
  must come from a strictly higher layer — a caller is expressed in its
  callee's language, so same-layer calls hide real strata (SL008).
- **Vocabulary is lenient**: references to same-file types, consts, and
  statics are legal at the referencer's own layer and below; only
  upward references violate (SL001).
- **Types sit at their minimal layer**: a type declared above the
  maximum layer its references justify is over-placed (SL009).
- Headings strictly ascend; **≤3 layers per file**. Wanting a fourth is
  the split signal — extract a module or crate, never flatten strata.
  Long call chains at one abstraction level are the same signal: split
  the chain into its own file, where it stratifies honestly.

## File anatomy

- A god-module splits into a **thin index** (rust.mdc's
  thin-orchestration-index: docs, `mod` decls, `pub use` re-exports —
  no logic, no headings) over one module per bounded operation, plus a
  shared-vocabulary leaf module for names two or more siblings need.
- Index files, test modules, and pure re-export files carry no headings
  and are skipped by the linter — a tooling adoption affordance, not an
  exemption: implementation modules MUST carry headings.
- Cross-module references are invisible to the per-file linter by
  design; the module DAG is enforced by rust.mdc crate structure and
  review, mirroring import-linter's role in Python rule 221.

## Enforcement

[`stratum-lint`](https://github.com/miniforge-ai/stratum-lint)'s Rust
implementation (`rust/`, cargo crate `stratum-lint-rs`):

```bash
cargo run -q -- src            # or: cargo install --git <repo> stratum-lint-rs
```

SL000 parse failure, SL001 upward reference (all first-segment path
refs count — functions passed as values, type references, `Self::x`
attributed to the impl's type), SL002 non-monotonic headings, SL003
layer budget, SL004 item before the first heading, SL008 same-layer
call, SL009 over-placed type. References inside macro invocation token
streams are not analyzed (syn limitation) — review covers them; a local
binding shadowing a same-file item name can false-positive — rename the
local.

## Exemplar

`minibench/crates/kernel` — a 2,454-line `lib.rs` split into a thin
index over nine Layer-headed modules (`snapshot`/`stats`/`provenance`/
`violations` vocabulary + `checks`/`summary`/`compare`/`regression`/
`evidence` operations), public API unchanged, all behavior tests
unmodified, linter clean under strict semantics. The split surfaced two
latent duplications (a copied helper, a thrice-repeated registry guard)
and four hidden same-layer call chains — the expected effect of making
strata explicit and checked.

## Examples

<example>
  Good — vocabulary below, orchestration above, guard mapped at the boundary:

  ```rust
  // ------------------------------------------------------------------ Layer 0
  pub(crate) fn registry_key(r: &StateVarRegistry) -> String {
      format!("{}@{}", r.registry_id, r.version)
  }
  // ------------------------------------------------------------------ Layer 1
  pub fn validate(snapshot: &Snapshot, registry: &StateVarRegistry) -> Result<Report, ValidateError> {
      check_registry(snapshot, registry).map_err(into_validate_error)?;
      // ...
  }
  ```
</example>

<example type="invalid">
  ```rust
  // BAD — Layer 0 helper reaching up into Layer 1
  // ------------------------------------------------------------------ Layer 0
  fn default_report() -> Report { assemble(&[]) }   // SL001
  // ------------------------------------------------------------------ Layer 1
  fn assemble(items: &[Item]) -> Report { /* ... */ }

  // BAD — four layers in one file: split the module instead   // SL003
  ```
</example>
